% Introduction
% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:symexec-intro}

Symbolic execution is a program analysis technique in which programs are executed
not with concrete input values, but with \emph{symbolic} values drawn from an
infinite domain.  Instead of producing a single output, a symbolically executed
program accumulates a set of \emph{path conditions}---logical formulae that
characterize the inputs under which each execution path is taken.  The result is
not a single trace but a \emph{tree of traces}, each annotated with constraints
on the program's inputs.

The technique was first described by King~\cite{king1976symbolic} in 1976 and
has since become one of the most powerful methods for automated program analysis,
test generation, and bug finding.  Modern symbolic execution engines such as
KLEE~\cite{cadar2008klee}, angr~\cite{shoshitaishvili2016angr}, and
S2E~\cite{chipounov2011s2e} have demonstrated the ability to find subtle bugs in
real-world software, including security vulnerabilities in operating system
kernels and file systems.

At its core, symbolic execution can be understood as a generalization of concrete
execution.  Where a concrete interpreter maps a program and an input to an
output, a symbolic interpreter maps a program to a \emph{symbolic summary}---a
disjunction of path conditions paired with symbolic outputs.  This perspective
reveals the deep connection between symbolic execution and the denotational
semantics studied in Part~III: a symbolic execution engine computes an
\emph{approximation} of the program's denotation, expressed as a formula in a
decidable fragment of first-order logic.

In this chapter we develop the theory and practice of symbolic execution in
depth.  We begin with the fundamental concepts of symbolic states and path
conditions (\cref{sec:symexec-states}), then proceed to the design of symbolic
execution engines (\cref{sec:symexec-engine}).  We examine the constraint
solving problem and the role of SMT solvers (\cref{sec:symexec-smt}), address
the path explosion problem and its mitigations (\cref{sec:symexec-path-explosion}),
and discuss concolic execution as a hybrid approach (\cref{sec:symexec-concolic}).
We conclude with applications and modern tools (\cref{sec:symexec-applications}).

% ============================================================
% Symbolic States and Path Conditions
% ============================================================
\section{Symbolic States and Path Conditions}\label{sec:symexec-states}

\subsection{Symbolic Values}\label{sec:symexec-symvals}

In concrete execution, each variable in a program is bound to a concrete value
from some domain---an integer, a floating-point number, a pointer, and so forth.
In symbolic execution, each variable is instead bound to a \emph{symbolic
expression}---a term in a first-order language.

\begin{definition}[Symbolic Value]\label{def:symval}
A \emph{symbolic value} $v$ is a term in a sorted first-order language
$\mathcal{L} = (\Sigma, \mathcal{S})$, where $\Sigma$ is a signature consisting
of function symbols and constant symbols, and $\mathcal{S}$ is a set of sorts.
We write $\mathcal{T}(\Sigma, \mathcal{V})$ for the set of all terms over
$\Sigma$ and a set of symbolic variables $\mathcal{V}$.
\end{definition}

For a simple integer language, the signature might include:
\[
\Sigma = \{0, 1, +, -, \times, \mathit{ite}, \mathit{read}, \mathit{write}, \ldots\}
\]
where $\mathit{ite}$ is the if-then-else function and $\mathit{read}$,
$\mathit{write}$ model memory operations.

\begin{example}[Symbolic Expressions]\label{ex:symexpr}
Consider the program fragment:
\begin{verbatim}
  x = input();
  y = x + 1;
  if (y > 10) {
    z = y * 2;
  } else {
    z = y - 1;
  }
\end{verbatim}
After the first two statements, the symbolic state maps $x \mapsto \alpha$ and
$y \mapsto \alpha + 1$, where $\alpha$ is a fresh symbolic variable representing
the unknown input.  At the branch, two paths emerge:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{True branch:} path condition $\alpha + 1 > 10$, with
      $z \mapsto (\alpha + 1) \times 2$.
\item \textbf{False branch:} path condition $\neg(\alpha + 1 > 10)$, with
      $z \mapsto (\alpha + 1) - 1$.
\end{itemize}
\end{example}

\subsection{Symbolic States}\label{sec:symexec-state-def}

A symbolic state captures the complete state of a symbolic execution at a given
program point.  It generalizes the concrete state by replacing concrete values
with symbolic expressions.

\begin{definition}[Symbolic State]\label{def:symstate}
A \emph{symbolic state} $\sigma$ is a triple $(\rho, \mu, \pi)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\rho : \mathit{Var} \to \mathcal{T}(\Sigma, \mathcal{V})$ is the
      \emph{symbolic environment}, mapping program variables to symbolic
      expressions;
\item $\mu : \mathcal{T}(\Sigma, \mathcal{V}) \to \mathcal{T}(\Sigma, \mathcal{V})$
      is the \emph{symbolic memory}, mapping symbolic addresses to symbolic
      values (for languages with heap memory);
\item $\pi \in \mathcal{F}(\Sigma, \mathcal{V})$ is the \emph{path condition},
      a first-order formula constraining the symbolic variables.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

The path condition $\pi$ is the conjunction of all branch conditions encountered
along the path from the program entry to the current point.  It represents the
necessary and sufficient condition on the input for execution to reach the
current state along this particular path.

\begin{definition}[Path Condition]\label{def:pathcond}
The \emph{path condition} $\pi$ at a program point $p$ along path $\omega$ is
defined inductively:
\begin{itemize}
\item At the entry point: $\pi_0 = \top$ (the trivially true formula).
\item At a conditional branch $\mathbf{if}\ e\ \mathbf{then}\ s_1\ \mathbf{else}\ s_2$:
  \begin{itemize}
  \item Taking the true branch: $\pi' = \pi \land \SemBrack{e}_\rho$
  \item Taking the false branch: $\pi' = \pi \land \neg\SemBrack{e}_\rho$
  \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
where $\SemBrack{e}_\rho$ denotes the symbolic evaluation of expression $e$
under environment $\rho$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Symbolic Evaluation Rules}\label{sec:symexec-rules}

The symbolic evaluation of a program is defined by a set of inference rules that
describe how each language construct transforms a symbolic state.

\begin{semrules}{Symbolic Evaluation}
  \frac{}{\config{x = e}{\sigma}{\pi} \longrightarrow \config{\mathbf{skip}}{\storeupdate{\rho}{x}{\SemBrack{e}_\rho}}{\pi}}
  \quad \text{(assignment)} \\[12pt]
  \frac{\SemBrack{e}_\rho = \top}{\config{\mathbf{if}\ e\ \mathbf{then}\ s_1\ \mathbf{else}\ s_2}{\sigma}{\pi} \longrightarrow \config{s_1}{\sigma}{\pi}}
  \quad \text{(branch, true)} \\[12pt]
  \frac{\SemBrack{e}_\rho = \bot}{\config{\mathbf{if}\ e\ \mathbf{then}\ s_1\ \mathbf{else}\ s_2}{\sigma}{\pi} \longrightarrow \config{s_2}{\sigma}{\pi}}
  \quad \text{(branch, false)} \\[12pt]
  \frac{\SemBrack{e}_\rho \neq \top \text{ and } \SemBrack{e}_\rho \neq \bot}{\config{\mathbf{if}\ e\ \mathbf{then}\ s_1\ \mathbf{else}\ s_2}{\sigma}{\pi} \longrightarrow \config{s_1}{\sigma}{\pi \land \SemBrack{e}_\rho} \;\mid\; \config{s_2}{\sigma}{\pi \land \neg\SemBrack{e}_\rho}}
  \quad \text{(fork)}
\end{semrules}

The \textsc{fork} rule is the heart of symbolic execution: when the branch
condition is neither trivially true nor trivially false (i.e., when the symbolic
expression is not a constant), execution \emph{splits} into two paths.  Each
path inherits the current symbolic state but extends the path condition with the
appropriate branch constraint.

\subsection{Feasibility and Satisfiability}\label{sec:symexec-feasibility}

Not every path through a program is \emph{feasible}---some paths may be
unreachable because their path conditions are unsatisfiable.  A fundamental
operation in symbolic execution is checking whether a path condition is
satisfiable.

\begin{definition}[Feasible Path]\label{def:feasible-path}
A path $\omega$ with path condition $\pi_\omega$ is \emph{feasible} if and only
if $\pi_\omega$ is satisfiable, i.e., there exists an assignment of concrete
values to the symbolic variables that makes $\pi_\omega$ true.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of Symbolic Execution]\label{thm:symexec-sound}
If a path $\omega$ is explored by symbolic execution and its path condition
$\pi_\omega$ is satisfiable, then there exists a concrete input that causes the
program to follow path $\omega$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By construction, the path condition $\pi_\omega$ is the conjunction of all branch
conditions along $\omega$.  If $\pi_\omega$ is satisfiable, any satisfying
assignment provides concrete values for the input variables that cause each
branch along $\omega$ to be taken in the specified direction.  By induction on
the length of the path, the concrete execution with these inputs follows $\omega$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Memory Modeling}\label{sec:symexec-memory}

Modeling memory in symbolic execution presents unique challenges.  There are two
principal approaches:

\paragraph{Concrete addressing.}
Memory is modeled as a map from concrete addresses to symbolic values.  This is
simple but cannot handle symbolic pointers---pointers whose values depend on
symbolic variables.

\paragraph{Symbolic addressing.}
Memory is modeled using the theory of arrays from SMT
logic~\cite{bradley2007arrays}.  The $\mathit{read}$ and $\mathit{write}$
functions satisfy the axioms:
\[
\mathit{read}(\mathit{write}(m, a, v), a) = v
\]
\[
a \neq b \implies \mathit{read}(\mathit{write}(m, a, v), b) = \mathit{read}(m, b)
\]
This approach handles symbolic pointers but produces more complex constraints.

\begin{insight}
The choice of memory model has a profound impact on the performance and
precision of symbolic execution.  Concrete addressing is faster but incomplete;
symbolic addressing is more precise but generates harder constraints.  Modern
engines typically use a hybrid approach, falling back to array-based reasoning
only when symbolic pointers are encountered.
\end{insight}

