\subsection{The Executor Component}\label{sec:symexec-executor}

The symbolic executor is the core component.  It processes instructions one at a
time, maintaining the current symbolic state and forking execution at branches.
The executor must handle:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Arithmetic operations:} Symbolic expressions are built by applying
      function symbols to sub-expressions.  For example, $x + y$ where both
      $x$ and $y$ are symbolic produces the expression $+(x, y)$.
\item \textbf{Memory operations:} Loads and stores are translated to
      $\mathit{read}$ and $\mathit{write}$ operations on the symbolic memory.
\item \textbf{Function calls:} Two strategies exist: \emph{inter-procedural}
      analysis (inlining or exploring called functions) and \emph{summary-based}
      analysis (using pre-computed function summaries).
\item \textbf{System calls and external functions:} These require special
      handling, as their semantics are not captured by the program text.  Engines
      typically provide \emph{environment models} that over-approximate the
      effects of external functions.
\end{itemize}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Core Symbolic Execution Loop}\label{alg:symexec-core}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Program $P$, initial symbolic state $\sigma_0$
\Ensure Set of path conditions and final states
\State $W \gets \{(\sigma_0, \mathit{entry})\}$ \Comment{Worklist}
\State $\mathit{Results} \gets \emptyset$
\While{$W \neq \emptyset$}
  \State $(\sigma, pc) \gets \mathit{select}(W)$ \Comment{Path selection strategy}
  \State $\mathit{instr} \gets \mathit{instruction\_at}(pc)$
  \If{$\mathit{instr}$ is \textsc{halt}}
    \State $\mathit{Results} \gets \mathit{Results} \cup \{(\sigma.\pi, \sigma.\rho)\}$
  \ElsIf{$\mathit{instr}$ is \textsc{branch} $e$}
    \State $v \gets \SemBrack{e}_{\sigma.\rho}$
    \If{$v = \top$}
      \State $W \gets W \cup \{(\sigma, pc_{\mathit{true}})\}$
    \ElsIf{$v = \bot$}
      \State $W \gets W \cup \{(\sigma, pc_{\mathit{false}})\}$
    \Else
      \State $\sigma_T \gets \sigma[\pi \mapsto \sigma.\pi \land v]$
      \State $\sigma_F \gets \sigma[\pi \mapsto \sigma.\pi \land \neg v]$
      \State $W \gets W \cup \{(\sigma_T, pc_{\mathit{true}}), (\sigma_F, pc_{\mathit{false}})\}$
    \EndIf
  \Else
    \State $\sigma' \gets \mathit{execute}(\mathit{instr}, \sigma)$
    \State $W \gets W \cup \{(\sigma', pc + 1)\}$
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\State \Return $\mathit{Results}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Path Selection Strategies}\label{sec:symexec-selection}

The order in which paths are explored from the worklist has a significant impact
on the effectiveness of symbolic execution.  Several strategies have been
proposed:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Depth-first search (DFS):} Explores one path as deeply as
      possible before backtracking.  Memory-efficient but may miss bugs on
      short paths.
\item \textbf{Breadth-first search (BFS):} Explores all paths uniformly.
      More thorough but requires storing many states.
\item \textbf{Shortest uncovered path first:} Prioritizes paths that have
      covered the fewest new instructions, aiming for broad code coverage.
\item \textbf{Random path selection:} Picks a random path from the worklist.
      Surprisingly effective in practice~\cite{cadar2008klee}.
\item \textbf{Coverage-optimized:} Uses heuristics to select paths likely to
      cover new code or reach new states.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{State Merging}\label{sec:symexec-merging}

When multiple execution paths converge at a join point (e.g., after an if-then-else
where both branches merge), the engine can \emph{merge} the symbolic states to
avoid redundant exploration.

\begin{definition}[State Merging]\label{def:state-merge}
Given two symbolic states $\sigma_1 = (\rho_1, \mu_1, \pi_1)$ and
$\sigma_2 = (\rho_2, \mu_2, \pi_2)$ at the same program point, the
\emph{merged state} $\sigma_{12} = (\rho_{12}, \mu_{12}, \pi_{12})$ is defined by:
\[
\rho_{12}(x) = \mathit{ite}(\pi_1, \rho_1(x), \rho_2(x)) \quad \text{for each variable } x
\]
\[
\pi_{12} = \top \quad \text{(or } \pi_1 \lor \pi_2 \text{ if merging is partial)}
\]
\end{definition}

State merging reduces the number of paths but produces more complex symbolic
expressions, creating a trade-off between path count and constraint complexity.

