% Constraint Solving and SMT
% ============================================================
% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Preserving Path Guards When Merging}

At a join, equal concrete values do not necessarily come from equal
symbolic environments, and exhausting both sides of a local branch does
not mean that the merged state represents all possible program inputs.
The distinction is easy to see when the branch occurs under an outer
precondition. An exact merge should carry that fact forward, even if
the immediate branch test no longer appears in the simplified path
condition.

\textbf{Contract.} Merge two feasible successors of a pure branch after
they reach the same program point. Their guards are disjoint over a
common input valuation, both stores bind the same live variables, and
there are no memory or external effects. Return a symbolic environment
that represents exactly the union of the two incoming sets of
input--store pairs. The \(\operatorname{ite}\) term denotes a
value-level conditional.

\begin{sudocode}[Merge a disjoint pair of states]
\Let{(p,L)}{\text{first guard and store}}
\Let{(q,R)}{\text{second guard and store}}
\To{The false arm is meaningful only under the union.}
\Let{g}{\Call{Simplify}{p\lor q}}
\Let{M}{\text{empty store}}
\For{each live name $x$}
  \If{$L[x]$ and $R[x]$ are structurally equal}
    \Let{M[x]}{L[x]}
  \Else
    \To{Select using the incoming guard, not a new input.}
    \Let{M[x]}{\operatorname{ite}(p,L[x],R[x])}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return $(g,M)$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} An expression evaluator assumes \(0\le x\le9\).
It sets \(y=x+2\) when \(x<4\), and \(y=2x\) otherwise.
The two guards reduce in union to \(0\le x\le9\), not to truth.
The merged expression yields \(5\) at \(x=3\) and \(8\) at \(x=4\).
At \(x=11\), the merged state represents no result, even though
the conditional term is capable of evaluating to \(22\).

For a valuation satisfying \(g\), exactly one incoming guard holds,
so all live values are selected consistently from that arm.
If \(g\) fails, neither incoming state applies. With shared expression
nodes the merge creates at most one conditional node per live name,
apart from guard construction and simplification. This bound does not
extend to later solver effort: many nested conditionals can make the
combined query more expensive than two separate ones.

\section{Constraint Solving and SMT}\label{sec:symexec-smt}

\subsection{The Role of SMT Solvers}\label{sec:symexec-smt-role}

At every branch, the symbolic execution engine must determine whether the path
condition is satisfiable.  If it is, the engine may also want to produce a
satisfying assignment (a concrete test input).  This is the task of an
\emph{SMT solver}---a decision procedure for satisfiability modulo theories.

Modern symbolic execution engines rely heavily on SMT solvers such as
Z3~\cite{demoura2008z3}, CVC4~\cite{barrett2011cvc4}, and
Boolector~\cite{brummayer2010boolector}.  These solvers decide the
satisfiability of formulae in combinations of decidable first-order theories,
including:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Theory of bit-vectors (BV):} Models fixed-width integers with
      overflow semantics, matching hardware arithmetic.
\item \textbf{Theory of arrays (AR):} Models memory with read/write operations.
\item \textbf{Theory of linear arithmetic (LA):} Models integer or real
      arithmetic with linear constraints.
\item \textbf{Theory of uninterpreted functions (UF):} Models functions with
      no assumed properties beyond congruence.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Theory Combinations}\label{sec:symexec-theories}

The Nelson--Oppen combination method~\cite{nelson1979nelson} allows SMT solvers
to decide satisfiability in the union of convex theories by exchanging equality
information between theory solvers.

\begin{theorem}[Nelson--Oppen Combination]\label{thm:nelson-oppen}
Let $T_1$ and $T_2$ be two stably infinite, decidable theories over disjoint
signatures $\Sigma_1$ and $\Sigma_2$.  Then satisfiability of quantifier-free
formulae in $T_1 \cup T_2$ is decidable.
\end{theorem}

For symbolic execution, the most common combination is $\mathrm{QF\_ABV}$---the
quantifier-free combination of arrays and bit-vectors.  This theory is expressive
enough to model most imperative programs with arrays and fixed-width arithmetic.

\subsection{Incremental Solving}\label{sec:symexec-incremental}

Path conditions along a single execution path differ by a single conjunct.
Rather than solving each path condition from scratch, modern engines use
\emph{incremental solving}: they push the current state to the solver, add the
new conjunct, check satisfiability, and pop back.

\begin{insight}
Incremental solving can yield order-of-magnitude speedups over non-incremental
approaches, because the solver retains learned clauses and internal state across
related queries.  This is one of the most important practical optimizations in
symbolic execution.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Solver Queries}\label{sec:symexec-queries}

Beyond satisfiability checking, symbolic execution engines issue several types
of queries to the solver:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Satisfiability:} Is $\pi$ satisfiable? (For branch feasibility.)
\item \textbf{Validity:} Is $\pi \implies \phi$ valid? (For assertion checking:
      does assertion $\phi$ hold on all feasible paths?)
\item \textbf{Model generation:} Given that $\pi$ is satisfiable, produce a
      satisfying assignment. (For test generation.)
\item \textbf{Unsat core extraction:} Given that $\pi$ is unsatisfiable, find a
      minimal subset of conjuncts responsible. (For debugging infeasible paths.)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Constraint Simplification}\label{sec:symexec-simplify}

Symbolic expressions can grow very large during execution.  Constraint
simplification is essential to keep solver queries manageable:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Constant folding:} Evaluate sub-expressions with all-concrete
      operands.
\item \textbf{Algebraic simplification:} Apply identities such as
      $x + 0 = x$, $x \times 1 = x$, $x - x = 0$.
\item \textbf{Expression hashing (hash-consing):} Ensure structurally identical
      sub-expressions share a single representation, enabling efficient equality
      checks and reducing memory usage.
\item \textbf{Rewriting:} Apply domain-specific rewrite rules, such as
      converting $\mathit{ite}(c, \mathit{ite}(c, a, b), d)$ to
      $\mathit{ite}(c, a, d)$.
\end{itemize}

