\section{Path Explosion and Mitigation}\label{sec:symexec-path-explosion}

\subsection{The Path Explosion Problem}\label{sec:symexec-explosion-def}

The most significant challenge in symbolic execution is the \emph{path explosion
problem}: the number of execution paths can grow exponentially with program size.
A program with $n$ independent boolean branches has $2^n$ paths; a loop with $k$
iterations and a branch in the body has $k+1$ paths per loop, compounding across
nested loops.

\begin{theorem}[Path Complexity]\label{thm:path-complexity}
For a program with $b$ conditional branches, all reachable and independent,
symbolic execution explores $2^b$ paths.  For a loop iterating at most $k$ times
with a conditional body, the number of paths is $\sum_{i=0}^{k} 2^i = 2^{k+1}-1$.
\end{theorem}

This exponential blowup means that exhaustive symbolic execution is infeasible
for all but the smallest programs.  The path explosion problem is not merely a
practical inconvenience; it reflects a fundamental computational barrier, as
symbolic execution subsumes the NP-hard satisfiability problem.

\subsection{Search Heuristics}\label{sec:symexec-heuristics}

Rather than exploring all paths, practical engines use heuristics to explore the
\emph{most interesting} paths first:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Coverage-guided search:} Prioritize paths that traverse
      uncovered basic blocks or branches.
\item \textbf{Bug-directed search:} Prioritize paths that approach potential
      error locations (e.g., null dereferences, buffer overflows).
\item \textbf{Diversity-based search:} Select paths whose constraints are
      maximally different from previously explored paths.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Constraint Strengthening and Weakening}\label{sec:symexec-strengthen}

\emph{Constraint strengthening} adds additional constraints to the path condition
to prune infeasible paths earlier.  For example, if a variable $x$ is known to
be in the range $[0, 100]$, adding $0 \leq x \leq 100$ to the path condition
helps the solver detect infeasibility sooner.

\emph{Constraint weakening} relaxes the path condition to enable merging of
paths.  This trades precision for tractability: the analysis becomes an
over-approximation, potentially reporting false positives but exploring fewer
paths.

\subsection{Loop Handling}\label{sec:symexec-loops}

Loops are a major source of path explosion.  A loop that iterates $k$ times
generates $k+1$ paths (for each possible number of iterations from 0 to $k$).
Several techniques address this:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Loop bounding:} Limit the number of loop iterations explored.
      This is sound for bug-finding (a bug that requires more iterations is not
      found, but no false positives are introduced) but incomplete for
      verification.
\item \textbf{Loop summarization:} Compute a symbolic summary of the loop body
      and apply it a symbolic number of times, using quantified formulae.
\item \textbf{Loop invariant generation:} Discover invariants that hold at the
      loop head, allowing the loop to be replaced by an assume statement.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{State Pruning}\label{sec:symexec-pruning}

Several techniques prune the set of explored states:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Subsumption:} If state $\sigma_1$ at program point $p$ has path
      condition $\pi_1$ and state $\sigma_2$ at the same point has $\pi_2$, and
      $\pi_1 \implies \pi_2$ and $\rho_1 = \rho_2$, then $\sigma_2$ is subsumed
      by $\sigma_1$ and can be discarded.
\item \textbf{Redundant state detection:} If two states are syntactically
      identical (same symbolic environment and same path condition modulo
      simplification), only one need be kept.
\item \textbf{Abstract symbolic execution:} Use abstract domains to merge
      similar states, as in abstract interpretation (see \cref{ch:ai-theory}).
\end{itemize}

