\section{Concolic Execution}\label{sec:symexec-concolic}

\subsection{Motivation}\label{sec:concolic-motivation}

Pure symbolic execution struggles with constraints that are difficult for SMT
solvers---for example, constraints involving complex nonlinear arithmetic,
cryptographic hash functions, or calls to unmodeled library functions.
\emph{Concolic execution} (a portmanteau of \emph{concrete} and
\emph{symbolic})~\cite{godefroid2005dart,sen2005cute} addresses this by running
the program both concretely and symbolically in parallel.

\subsection{The Concolic Approach}\label{sec:concolic-approach}

In concolic execution, each symbolic variable is paired with a concrete value.
The concrete values define a specific execution path; the symbolic expressions
track the constraints along that path.  When the concrete execution encounters a
branch, the engine:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Records the branch condition symbolically.
\item Follows the branch indicated by the concrete value.
\item After the execution completes, negates one of the recorded branch
      conditions and solves for new concrete inputs.
\item Re-executes with the new inputs.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{definition}[Concolic State]\label{def:concolic-state}
A \emph{concolic state} is a pair $(\sigma_c, \sigma_s)$ where $\sigma_c$ is a
concrete state (mapping variables to concrete values) and $\sigma_s$ is a
symbolic state (mapping variables to symbolic expressions), such that for each
variable $x$, the concrete value $\sigma_c(x)$ is a \emph{realization} of the
symbolic expression $\sigma_s(x)$ under the current input assignment.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Concolic Execution}\label{alg:concolic}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Program $P$, initial concrete input $\vec{c}_0$
\Ensure Set of test inputs covering different paths
\State $\vec{c} \gets \vec{c}_0$
\State $\mathit{Inputs} \gets \{\vec{c}_0\}$
\Loop
  \State Execute $P$ concretely with $\vec{c}$, tracking symbolic path condition $\pi$
  \State Let $\pi = c_1 \land c_2 \land \cdots \land c_n$ be the branch conditions
  \State $\mathit{found} \gets \mathit{false}$
  \For{$i = n$ down to $1$} \Comment{Try negating from the end}
    \State $\pi' \gets c_1 \land \cdots \land c_{i-1} \land \neg c_i$
    \If{$\mathrm{SMT.Solve}(\pi') = \mathit{sat}$ with model $\vec{c}'$}
      \State $\vec{c} \gets \vec{c}'$
      \State $\mathit{Inputs} \gets \mathit{Inputs} \cup \{\vec{c}\}$
      \State $\mathit{found} \gets \mathit{true}$
      \State \textbf{break}
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \If{$\neg \mathit{found}$}
    \State \textbf{break} \Comment{All paths explored}
  \EndIf
\EndLoop
\State \Return $\mathit{Inputs}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Advantages of Concolic Execution}\label{sec:concolic-advantages}

Concolic execution offers several advantages over pure symbolic execution:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Handling of complex operations:} When the symbolic path becomes
      too complex for the solver, the concrete execution can still proceed,
      using the concrete values to resolve branches.
\item \textbf{No path explosion from concrete-determined branches:} Branches
      whose conditions evaluate to concrete values do not cause forking.
\item \textbf{Natural test generation:} Each concrete execution is a valid test
      case; the negated constraints generate new, diverse test inputs.
\item \textbf{Graceful degradation:} If the solver fails on a constraint, the
      concrete execution continues with the concrete value.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{DART and CUTE}\label{sec:concolic-dart-cute}

The first concolic execution systems were DART (Directed Automated Random
Testing)~\cite{godefroid2005dart} and CUTE (Concolic Unit Testing
Engine)~\cite{sen2005cute}.  DART interleaves random testing with symbolic
execution: when symbolic execution gets stuck, it falls back to random input
generation.  CUTE focuses on C programs and handles pointer-based data structures
by generating inputs that exercise different structural configurations.

\subsection{KLEE and SAGE}\label{sec:concolic-klee-sage}

KLEE~\cite{cadar2008klee} operates on LLVM bitcode and performs pure symbolic
execution (not concolic), but uses many of the same techniques.  It has been
used to find bugs in GNU core utilities and device drivers.

SAGE~\cite{godefroid2008sage}, developed at Microsoft, is a concolic execution
engine that has been used to find security vulnerabilities in Windows.  SAGE
runs programs at the x86 binary level, using a white-box fuzzing approach that
has discovered hundreds of bugs in production software.

