\section{Applications}\label{sec:symexec-applications}

\subsection{Test Generation}\label{sec:symexec-testgen}

One of the most successful applications of symbolic execution is automated test
generation.  The key insight is that each satisfiable path condition, together
with its satisfying assignment, constitutes a test case that exercises a
specific path through the program.

\begin{definition}[Path Coverage]\label{def:path-coverage}
A test suite $T$ \emph{achieves path coverage} for program $P$ if for every
feasible path $\omega$ of $P$, there exists a test input $t \in T$ such that
executing $P$ on $t$ follows path $\omega$.
\end{definition}

Symbolic execution can achieve path coverage (modulo the path explosion problem)
by systematically exploring all feasible paths and generating a test input for
each.  In practice, engines aim for weaker coverage criteria such as branch
coverage or condition coverage, which are more tractable.

\subsection{Bug Finding}\label{sec:symexec-bugs}

Symbolic execution is particularly effective at finding specific classes of bugs:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Assertion violations:} At each $\mathbf{assert}(\phi)$, check
      whether $\pi \land \neg\phi$ is satisfiable.
\item \textbf{Null pointer dereferences:} At each pointer dereference $*p$,
      check whether $\pi \land p = 0$ is satisfiable.
\item \textbf{Buffer overflows:} At each array access $a[i]$, check whether
      $\pi \land (i < 0 \lor i \geq |a|)$ is satisfiable.
\item \textbf{Division by zero:} At each division $x / y$, check whether
      $\pi \land y = 0$ is satisfiable.
\item \textbf{Integer overflows:} Check whether arithmetic operations can
      overflow their result type.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Verification}\label{sec:symexec-verification}

Symbolic execution can be used for \emph{bounded verification}: verifying that a
program is correct up to a given bound on path length or loop iterations.  If
all explored paths satisfy the specification, the program is verified \emph{up
to that bound}.  This is weaker than full verification but can still provide
strong guarantees.

For programs with finite state spaces (e.g., programs operating on bounded
inputs), symbolic execution can achieve \emph{full verification} by exhaustively
exploring all paths.

\subsection{Exploit Generation}\label{sec:symexec-exploit}

In security applications, symbolic execution can be used to generate
\emph{exploits}---concrete inputs that trigger a vulnerability.  Given a program
and a vulnerability condition (e.g., ``the return address on the stack is
overwritten''), symbolic execution finds an input that satisfies the
vulnerability condition, effectively constructing an exploit.

