\section{Modern Tools}\label{sec:symexec-tools}

\subsection{KLEE}\label{sec:symexec-klee}

KLEE~\cite{cadar2008klee} is a symbolic execution engine operating on LLVM
bitcode.  Key features include:
\begin{itemize}
\item Operates on unmodified LLVM bitcode of C and C++ programs.
\item Uses the STP (and later Z3) SMT solver for constraint solving.
\item Supports the POSIX environment model for system calls.
\item Generates test cases as input files for the program under test.
\item Has found thousands of bugs in real-world software.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{angr}\label{sec:symexec-angr}

angr~\cite{shoshitaishvili2016angr} is a binary analysis platform that includes
a symbolic execution engine.  Key features include:
\begin{itemize}
\item Operates on raw binaries (no source code required).
\item Uses the VEX intermediate representation for cross-architecture support.
\item Provides a Python API for scripting analyses.
\item Supports both symbolic execution and static analysis (value-set analysis,
      dependency analysis).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{S2E}\label{sec:symexec-s2e}

S2E~\cite{chipounov2011s2e} is a platform for in-vivo symbolic execution,
running the target program inside a virtual machine alongside the symbolic
execution engine.  This allows analysis of complete software stacks, including
operating system kernels, device drivers, and applications.

\subsection{Other Notable Systems}\label{sec:symexec-other}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{ExeGlot} and \textbf{Otter}: Early symbolic execution systems.
\item \textbf{jCUTE} and \textbf{Java PathFinder}: Symbolic execution for Java.
\item \textbf{Pex}: Microsoft's symbolic execution framework for .NET.
\item \textbf{Mayhem}: Combines symbolic execution with directed fuzzing for
      automated exploit generation.
\item \textbf{Driller}: Combines AFL (a fuzzer) with angr (a symbolic executor)
      to overcome the limitations of each approach alone.
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
Symbolic execution was first proposed by King~\cite{king1976symbolic} in 1976,
contemporaneously with the development of dataflow analysis and abstract
interpretation.  The technique remained largely theoretical for decades due to
the lack of efficient constraint solvers.  The development of modern SMT solvers
in the 2000s---particularly Z3~\cite{demoura2008z3}---transformed symbolic
execution from a theoretical curiosity into a practical tool capable of analyzing
real-world software.  The KLEE paper~\cite{cadar2008klee} is widely regarded as
the work that demonstrated the practical viability of symbolic execution for bug
finding in systems software.
\end{historical}

