% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:symexec-summary}

Symbolic execution is a powerful technique for program analysis that bridges the
gap between testing and verification.  By executing programs with symbolic inputs
and accumulating path conditions, it can systematically explore the space of
program behaviors and generate test cases, find bugs, or verify properties.

The key challenges remain the path explosion problem and the cost of constraint
solving.  Active research areas include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Compositional symbolic execution:} Analyzing functions
      independently and composing results to scale to large programs.
\item \textbf{Machine learning for path selection:} Using learned models to
      guide the exploration toward interesting paths.
\item \textbf{Parallel symbolic execution:} Distributing the exploration across
      multiple cores or machines.
\item \textbf{Integration with fuzzing:} Combining the systematic exploration of
      symbolic execution with the scalability of fuzz testing.
\end{itemize}

The reader interested in deeper treatment should consult the comprehensive survey
by Baldoni et al.~\cite{baldoni2018survey} and the lecture notes by
Bratberg~\cite{bratberg2020symexec}.
